 ### Backup and Restore
 
 **Automatic backups**: Decenza backs up shots, settings, profiles, and media daily, and the backups survive app reinstalls. On **Settings → History & Data** you can set the **Backup Time**, see when the next one runs, and restore by picking a dated entry from **Restore from Backup**. Backups are written to `Documents/Decenza Backups/` and kept for 5 days. **Backup Now** runs one immediately.
 
-**Manual backup**: Web interface burger menu → **Backup** → download a `.zip` file.
+**Save a backup**: In **Settings → History & Data**, choose a dated backup and tap **Save Backup to This Device**. In a browser, the ZIP downloads to the computer running that browser; the host's backup stays in place. Use **Cancel** to abandon the download. Backup downloads have no fixed size limit; available storage still applies.
 
-**Restore**: Web interface burger menu → **Restore** → upload a `.zip` → select which data types to restore.
+**Restore**: In **Settings → History & Data**, select a dated backup or tap **Restore Backup from This Device** to choose a `.zip`, `.dcbackup`, or older `.db` backup. Confirm the data categories and whether to merge or replace before starting. Browser files come from the computer running that browser and are restored on the connected Decenza host.
 
-Restoring merges by shot, so shots you already have are not duplicated and AI chat history keeps pointing at the right ones. **If a restore says it was refused**, nothing was changed — it could not tell which shots you already had, so it stopped rather than duplicate them.
+Restoring merges by shot, so shots you already have are not duplicated and AI chat history keeps pointing at the right ones. **If shot history restore is refused**, existing shots remain unchanged. Other selected categories may already have been restored; read the result before retrying.
 
 **Export Shots to File** (*Mirror shots to JSON files*) writes each shot as Visualizer-format JSON into the history folder alongside your profiles. These files are for your own archive — the app never reads them back.
 
